# Host-level shared edge (#310, option A): Caddy owns 80/443 for EVERY tenant.
# Each tenant is a loopback upstream on its registered port (see ports.md).
# The edge forwards to the tenant's HTTPS port — its HTTP port only redirects (#310
# redirect-loop finding). The tenant presents a self-signed cert on the internal
# hop, hence insecure_skip_verify on loopback only.
#
# COMMITTED AS CODE since #338: /etc/caddy/Caddyfile on the host must be
# byte-identical to this file — `infra/edge/apply.sh --check` diffs them, and
# `apply.sh` is the only sanctioned way to change the running edge. The edge
# deliberately does NOT roll on tenant deploys (the option-A rationale): apply
# is a manual, reviewed step. No secrets live here: hostnames + loopback ports.

# The mail server's hostname (MX target, IMAP/SMTP TLS name). Served here ONLY
# so the edge — the one ACME client on this host — obtains and renews its
# certificate; the `mailserver` compose service mounts that certificate
# directory read-only. Nothing web-facing lives behind it.
mail.beaconfolio.com {
	respond "mail.beaconfolio.com: IMAP 993, SMTP submission 465/587" 200
}

beaconfolio.com, www.beaconfolio.com {
	reverse_proxy https://127.0.0.1:18443 {
		transport http {
			tls
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
		header_up Host {host}
	}
}

admin.beaconfolio.com {
	reverse_proxy https://127.0.0.1:18443 {
		transport http {
			tls
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
		header_up Host {host}
	}
}

# mavrov.de is the owner's personal domain and only REDIRECTS to beaconfolio.com — de-brand:canonical: the owner's personal domain, kept only as a redirect to the product
# (owner, 2026-10-11). No upstream and no port: Caddy answers it itself, with a
# certificate of its own obtained automatically (the DNS zone is at Hetzner and
# points both names at this host). 301, path and query kept.
mavrov.de, www.mavrov.de { # de-brand:canonical: the owner's personal domain, kept only as a redirect to the product
	redir https://beaconfolio.com{uri} permanent
}

# --- Tenant: viafrei (ports.md block 18100–18199) ----------------------------
# Plain-HTTP loopback upstreams: the tenant terminates nothing itself (no
# self-signed hop, so no tls_insecure_skip_verify and no redirect-only port).
# viafrei.de/www serve a static landing page; mcp.viafrei.de is an MCP
# Streamable HTTP server (long-lived SSE responses — no buffering).

# viafrei.de is PUBLIC and serves ONE page to everybody: the live national
# digest on :18180 (viafrei repo issue #176, owner 2026-09-20).
#
# It used to fork on the owner's address — :18181 for the owner, :18180 for
# everybody else — from the days when :18180 was the "im Aufbau" notice and
# :18181 was the private preview of the real page. #176 put the live digest on
# :18180, and the fork inverted without anyone editing it: a stranger got the
# product and the owner got the building site. Measured before removing it,
# from two addresses: 23 840 bytes and "live in Deutschland" from off-host,
# 2 160 bytes and "Vorschau" from the owner's.
#
# That is the failure a routing fork invites — its two sides are named by
# what they were, and one of them changed underneath the name. There is
# nothing left to preview privately, so the fork is gone rather than swapped.
#
# :18181 still exists in the tenant's compose and still serves the old notice;
# nothing routes to it. Retiring the port is a viafrei-repo change and belongs
# with the v0.1.0 landing work.
#
# BEHIND CLOUDFLARE (viafrei repo issue #1267, owner 2026-11-09: protection
# from crawlers that ignore robots.txt). viafrei.de and www.viafrei.de are the
# ONLY two names on this host whose DNS records are Cloudflare-PROXIED; every
# other name (mcp., status., admin., mail. and every beaconfolio site) stays a
# plain A/AAAA record and its block below does not know Cloudflare exists.
# Public still means public: everybody reaches the site — through Cloudflare.
#
#   * A request is served THROUGH THE GATE when it matches @cf_visitor: its
#     connection comes from a Cloudflare address AND it carries a
#     CF-Connecting-IP that looks like an address (Cloudflare sets that header
#     on every request it forwards; without it the tenant would get an empty
#     X-Forwarded-For). Then the apex reverse-proxies to :18180 with Host
#     passed through and CF-Connecting-IP as X-Forwarded-For, and www
#     redirects permanently to the apex.
#   * ANY OTHER REQUEST IS SERVED TOO, the pre-Cloudflare way (2026-11-09,
#     owner-approved hotfix). The first cut answered it 403, so a crawler could
#     not skip Cloudflare with `Host: viafrei.de` straight to this host (one
#     curl --resolve) — but a resolver still caching the old direct record
#     sends REAL visitors here too (Google DNS still did 98 min after the
#     switch; the owner could not open the site from a tablet), and a 403 to
#     direct visitors broke the site for anyone with a stale DNS answer. So
#     the bare `handle {` fallback proxies the apex to the same upstream with
#     Host passed through and NO address header from the request (Caddy sets
#     X-Forwarded-For to the direct peer, which a caller cannot choose — true
#     only while NO trusted_proxies is configured anywhere, which apply.test.sh
#     asserts), and
#     www's fallback redirects to the apex. The Cloudflare path still carries
#     the real client address; the crawler bypass is open again, and closing
#     it needs Authenticated Origin Pulls (below), not a 403 to people.
#   * The apex hands the tenant the VISITOR's address as X-Forwarded-For,
#     taken from CF-Connecting-IP — OVERWRITTEN, never appended, and ONLY
#     inside `handle @cf_visitor`. Cloudflare overwrites CF-Connecting-IP on
#     every request it forwards, so on a Cloudflare connection the header is
#     Cloudflare's statement and not the caller's; on a direct connection it
#     would be whatever the caller typed, and the tenant's rate limit and place
#     lookup would believe it. Without the header the tenant would see one
#     Cloudflare PoP for every visitor.
#
# WHAT remote_ip DOES NOT PROVE. A Cloudflare address says the connection
# came through Cloudflare's network — not that it came through OUR zone. Any
# Cloudflare customer can point a proxied record of their own at this host's
# address, and their traffic then passes this gate with their zone's settings
# (none of our crawler protection) and a CF-Connecting-IP Cloudflare set for
# their visitor. Since the 2026-11-09 hotfix the gate decides only WHICH
# address the tenant is handed (CF-Connecting-IP, or the direct peer); it
# refuses nobody. The stronger follow-up is Authenticated Origin
# Pulls: Cloudflare presents a client certificate on the origin connection and
# this edge requires it (`tls { client_auth { mode require_and_verify,
# trust_pool file <origin-pull CA> } }` on these two blocks). Note that the
# shared Cloudflare origin-pull CA proves "Cloudflare" again; only a
# per-zone/per-hostname certificate proves "our zone".
#
# THE RANGES ARE NOT IN THIS FILE, for the same reason the allow-list below is
# not: this file carries no address literal (apply.test.sh refuses one), and
# the list changes on Cloudflare's schedule, not on a code review. `import`
# reads one line, INSIDE the @cf_visitor matcher, from a host-side file written
# by a committed generator:
#
#     /etc/caddy/viafrei-cloudflare.conf   (root:caddy, 0640), one line:
#         remote_ip <cidr> [<cidr> …]
#
#     sudo bash infra/edge/cloudflare-ranges.sh --write   # fetch, validate, install
#     sudo bash infra/edge/cloudflare-ranges.sh --check   # read-only: current?
#
# The generator fetches https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and /ips-v6, refuses
# any line that is not a CIDR and a list shorter than 10 IPv4 or 5 IPv6
# ranges (exit 2: a truncated download must never become "nobody may reach
# the site"), writes atomically and keeps the previous file as .bak. A
# refreshed list needs a validate + reload; apply.sh does not reload an edge
# whose Caddyfile is already in sync. The daily drift check is the systemd
# pair in infra/edge/systemd/ (viafrei-cloudflare-ranges-check.{service,timer}):
# it runs --check, logs to the journal and FAILS the unit when Cloudflare's
# lists moved; it never writes. WHO READS A FAILED UNIT: the 3-hourly prod
# round, `systemctl is-failed viafrei-cloudflare-ranges-check.service` (a
# finding when it answers `failed`); `systemctl --failed` shows it to anyone
# on the host.
#
# apply.sh also reads the file's WIDTH: a /0, an IPv4 prefix wider than /8, an
# IPv6 prefix wider than /12 or a token that is no address (`private_ranges`)
# is refused by name — it would validate, and let far more than Cloudflare in.
#
# CERTIFICATES BEHIND CLOUDFLARE. This edge keeps obtaining and renewing the
# viafrei.de and www.viafrei.de certificates itself; Cloudflare's own
# certificate is what visitors see, ours is what Full (strict) verifies.
#   - TLS-ALPN-01 stops working: Cloudflare terminates TLS, the CA's ALPN
#     handshake never reaches us. Caddy logs that attempt failing and retries
#     with HTTP-01 — expected noise, not a fault.
#   - HTTP-01 goes through Cloudflare. Always Use HTTPS is ON in the zone, so
#     the CA's http://…/.well-known/acme-challenge/<t> is answered at the edge
#     of Cloudflare with a 301 to https (the CA follows it), and arrives HERE on
#     443 — with a Cloudflare peer and a CF-Connecting-IP header, inside the
#     very blocks below. Caddy answers a challenge in Server.ServeHTTP BEFORE
#     any route ("guarantee ACME HTTP challenges; handle them separately from
#     any user-defined handlers", modules/caddyhttp/server.go, v2.11.9), so it
#     is never proxied or redirected — not even for a peer outside the ranges.
#     Proved end to end by infra/edge/acme-behind-cloudflare.test.sh (docker,
#     a test CA, a 301-only port 80, ALPN off, a peer outside the ranges:
#     both names renewed through 443).
#   - This works only while the CURRENT certificate is valid (Full (strict)
#     needs it to reach us at all). Caddy renews with a third of the lifetime
#     left — about 30 days of retries for a 90-day certificate.
#   - CHECK after the switch, and at every prod round: the notAfter of the
#     certificate the ORIGIN presents (not Cloudflare's),
#       echo | openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:443 -servername viafrei.de 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate
#     (and the same for www.viafrei.de) must stay more than 20 days out, and
#     `journalctl -u caddy | grep 'certificate renewed successfully'` names
#     both after each renewal. Under 20 days is a finding.
#   - FALLBACK when renewal through Cloudflare fails: either switch the two
#     records back to DNS-only for the renewal (the ROLLBACK order below; the
#     edge then renews directly), or install a Cloudflare Origin CA
#     certificate (15 years, trusted by Cloudflare only — fine under Full
#     (strict), useless DNS-only) as a `tls <cert> <key>` in both blocks.
#
# The file must exist BEFORE the first apply: `caddy validate` fails on an
# import that matches no file, and apply.sh refuses by name before it gets
# there. A file WITHOUT a remote_ip line is refused too — inside the matcher
# it would leave only the header test, and a header is what a direct caller
# can send. A missing file therefore means "the change does not happen", never
# "the site is open to every crawler" nor "the site is shut".
#
# ORDER ON THE DAY, switching ON:
#   0. the zone is on Cloudflare's nameservers (done 2026-11-09) and its
#      SSL/TLS mode is Full (strict) (set, 2026-11-09). Flexible would send
#      Cloudflare to this edge over http, which redirects to https: a loop.
#      Nothing at Cloudflare may rewrite the origin request to http.
#   1. switch the two records to proxied in Cloudflare;
#   2. wait out the OLD records' TTL (auto, about 300 s) — a resolver may
#      hold the direct address far longer (98 min measured), which is why a
#      direct connection is served, not refused — or lower the TTL a day ahead;
#   3. write the ranges file, then apply this Caddyfile.
# Applied while the records are still DNS-only, every visitor connects
# directly and is served by the fallback, with its own address as
# X-Forwarded-For.
# ROLLBACK is the reverse: apply the previous Caddyfile FIRST (the site serves
# every connection again), THEN switch the records back to DNS-only.
viafrei.de {
	@cf_visitor {
		import /etc/caddy/viafrei-cloudflare.conf
		header_regexp CF-Connecting-IP ^[0-9A-Fa-f.:]+$
	}
	handle @cf_visitor {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18180 {
			header_up Host {host}
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.CF-Connecting-IP}
		}
	}
	# DIRECT VISITORS ARE SERVED (2026-11-09, owner-approved hotfix): a
	# resolver that still caches the pre-Cloudflare A record sends its users
	# here directly (Google DNS still did 98 min after the switch), and a 403
	# to direct visitors broke the site for every one of them. The
	# pre-Cloudflare shape: Host passed through, and NO address header taken
	# from the request -- Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For to the direct peer
	# itself, so a caller cannot choose it. Only the @cf_visitor path above
	# trusts CF-Connecting-IP and carries the real client address.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18180 {
			header_up Host {host}
		}
	}
}

# www.viafrei.de REDIRECTS to the apex (viafrei repo, 2026-10-12). It used to
# share the block above and serve the site itself, so Google indexed
# https://www.viafrei.de/ as its own canonical page beside https://viafrei.de/de
# (Search Console URL inspection, 2026-10-12) — a duplicate of the home page.
# Permanent, path and query kept; the certificate for www stays automatic.
# Behind Cloudflare like the apex (#1267), with the same matcher. A direct
# connection (a resolver still caching the pre-Cloudflare record) gets the SAME
# redirect, never a 403: the 403 broke the site for every stale DNS answer
# (2026-11-09, owner-approved hotfix). The apex it lands on serves it either way.
www.viafrei.de {
	@cf_visitor {
		import /etc/caddy/viafrei-cloudflare.conf
		header_regexp CF-Connecting-IP ^[0-9A-Fa-f.:]+$
	}
	handle @cf_visitor {
		redir https://viafrei.de{uri} permanent
	}
	handle {
		redir https://viafrei.de{uri} permanent
	}
}

# ACCESS LOG for mcp.viafrei.de ONLY (viafrei repo issue #438, that tenant's
# owner 2026-10-04). Purpose: tell "slow before the app" from "slow in the
# app" for multi-second stalls — Caddy's JSON line carries `duration` (the
# whole request as the edge saw it) and `status`, and the MCP server logs its
# own per-request time, so the two can be laid side by side. No other site
# block on this host logs (apply.test.sh asserts it), and this block changes
# nothing about them.
#
# HEADERS ARE AN ALLOW-LIST, not a deny-list. `request>headers delete` and
# `resp_headers delete` drop both header maps whole, and `log_append` puts back
# exactly three request headers as top-level fields. A deny-list cannot be
# complete: any header a client invents (X-Forwarded-For, Forwarded,
# X-Real-IP, True-Client-IP, CF-Connecting-IP, Referer, an API key under a
# custom name) would be written verbatim, unmasked (#477 review round 1).
# Dropped with the maps: Authorization, Cookie, Set-Cookie and Mcp-Session-Id
# in both directions. The legacy `/messages` endpoint carries the MCP session
# in the `sessionId` query parameter; it is deleted from `uri`.
#
# The client address is truncated to its /24 (IPv4) or /48 (IPv6) on BOTH
# fields that carry it (GDPR art. 5(1)(c), minimised at write time).
#
# RETENTION, measured on caddy 2.11.4: the active file rotates when it reaches
# roll_size OR when roll_interval has passed — but only at the next WRITE;
# Caddy has no timer, so with no requests nothing rotates and nothing is
# pruned (nor is anything new written). roll_keep_for counts from a rotated
# file's ROTATION time and is enforced at rotations, so with daily rotation a
# line is deleted at the latest ~24h (active) + 120h (kept) + 24h (the next
# rotation that prunes) = 168h after it was written, as long as the endpoint
# gets a request at least once a day. Rotated files are gzipped. Disk bound:
# at most roll_keep + 1 files of roll_size (about 400 MiB, far less gzipped).
#
# The file must belong to the service user: apply.sh validates AS that user
# and refuses an existing log file owned by anyone else.
mcp.viafrei.de {
	log_append user_agent {http.request.header.User-Agent}
	log_append accept {http.request.header.Accept}
	log_append content_type {http.request.header.Content-Type}
	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/viafrei-mcp-access.log {
			roll_size 50MiB
			roll_interval 24h
			roll_keep 7
			roll_keep_for 120h
		}
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>remote_ip ip_mask {
					ipv4 24
					ipv6 48
				}
				request>client_ip ip_mask {
					ipv4 24
					ipv6 48
				}
				request>headers delete
				resp_headers delete
				request>uri query {
					delete sessionId
				}
			}
		}
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18187 {
		header_up Host {host}
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

# The viafrei tenant's OPERATIONS CONSOLE (viafrei repo issues #94, #560) — one
# of the TWO site blocks on this host gated on the owner's addresses (the other
# is the status page, the next block), and deliberately so: the blocks above
# serve the product and stay PUBLIC (viafrei owner, 2026-09-19; viafrei.de and
# www. are gated on CLOUDFLARE's ranges, which is not a restriction on who may
# read them, only on the road they take — #1267), this one serves the
# operational surface — every worker's effective configuration, every feed's
# errors, the schema head, what is deployed, and since viafrei 2.0 the owner's
# restart and configuration actions. It moved from status.viafrei.de to
# admin.viafrei.de in viafrei 2.0 (owner, 2026-10-11/12).
#
# THE ADDRESSES ARE NOT IN THIS FILE, and must never be. They are personal data,
# they change when a network does, and changing them must not require a code
# review. `import` reads one line from a file the owner writes on the host,
# outside every repository:
#
#     /etc/caddy/viafrei-status-allow.conf   (root:caddy, 0640), one line:
#         @allowed remote_ip <cidr> [<cidr> …]
#
# 0640 root:caddy, not 0644: the contents are the owner's home and office
# addresses — personal data — and this box runs a second tenant's processes.
# Caddy reads the file as its own group; nothing else on the host needs to.
#
# FIRST RUN, in this order (the file must exist BEFORE the first apply, because
# `caddy validate` fails on an import that matches no file — which looks like a
# broken config and is only a missing step):
#   1. create /etc/caddy/viafrei-status-allow.conf with the `@allowed remote_ip
#      …` line, then `chown root:caddy` + `chmod 0640` it;
#   2. `bash infra/edge/apply.sh`          (read-only check + diff)
#   3. `bash infra/edge/apply.sh --apply`  (validate, then reload)
#
# DNS: `admin.viafrei.de` and `status.viafrei.de` must be plain A/AAAA records,
# NEVER CDN-proxied ones. `remote_ip` matches the DIRECT peer, so behind a proxy
# these blocks would see the proxy's address and the restriction would silently
# become "nobody" — or, if that range were ever listed, "everybody".
#
# That file is NOT part of apply.sh's byte-identical diff (apply.sh compares
# THIS file with /etc/caddy/Caddyfile), so editing the address list is one edit
# plus a reload — no PR, no deploy. When it is missing or empty, `caddy
# validate` fails, `apply.sh` refuses and the running edge keeps serving: the
# failure mode is "the change does not happen", never "the page became public".
#
# Layer two is the app itself: viafrei's `status` container enforces the same
# list from STATUS_ALLOW_CIDR (empty = deny all), and the header below is what
# tells it who is asking. It is OVERWRITTEN and not appended: Caddy appends by
# default, and an appended header would let a client choose its own source
# address for that second layer.
#
# The container is behind a published loopback port, so the peer address it
# sees is docker's bridge gateway and never this proxy's loopback address —
# which is why it believes the header from that RFC 1918 range
# (STATUS_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR, viafrei's deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml) and
# reads the LAST hop, the one written here. Nothing on that path is configured
# in this repository; the only contract is: overwrite, never append.
admin.viafrei.de {
	import /etc/caddy/viafrei-status-allow.conf
	handle @allowed {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18190 {
			header_up Host {host}
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		}
	}
	handle {
		respond 403
	}
}

# The viafrei tenant's STATUS PAGE (viafrei repo issue #589): status.viafrei.de,
# served by the SAME container as the console above. It was public from viafrei
# 2.0 (owner, 2026-10-11) until 2026-10-13, when the owner closed it again:
# "Close the status panel by the ip white list again, it should be available
# only to our laptop". Since then it is gated by the SAME allow-list file as the
# console, in exactly the same shape — import, `handle @allowed`, and a bare
# refusal for everybody else — and the same DNS rule applies to it. The refusal
# here is `410`, not the console's `403` (viafrei owner, 2026-10-18): Google had
# indexed this host while it was public and Search Console reported its URLs as
# "Blocked due to access forbidden (403)"; 410 Gone tells a crawler the pages are
# gone for good, so they drop out of the index instead of being retried.
#
# The app still routes the two names to DIFFERENT routers: the container routes
# by Host, and on this name it reaches only the public router, which holds no
# operations pool and answers a console path with 404. The gate here does not
# replace that separation and that separation does not replace the gate. The two
# names stay two blocks rather than one with a per-host matcher, so an edit to
# one block can never drop the other's import. Host is passed through because
# the router keys on it; X-Forwarded-For is overwritten for the reason above.
status.viafrei.de {
	import /etc/caddy/viafrei-status-allow.conf
	handle @allowed {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18190 {
			header_up Host {host}
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		}
	}
	handle {
		respond 410
	}
}

# --- Second-tenant template (#338 AC: the dry-run example) -------------------
# Onboarding a tenant is a PR against THIS file + ports.md, then one apply:
#   1. Claim a port range in ports.md (next free 100-block).
#   2. Uncomment + adapt the block below; the upstream port is the tenant's
#      registered HTTPS publish (its compose binds 127.0.0.1:<port>:443).
#   3. PR + review; on the host: `apply.sh` (read-only check), `apply.sh --apply`.
#
# nextproject.example {
# 	reverse_proxy https://127.0.0.1:18543 {
# 		transport http {
# 			tls
# 			tls_insecure_skip_verify
# 		}
# 		header_up Host {host}
# 	}
# }
